---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../data/site';
import { decomposition, measurements, environment, fine } from '../data/measurements';
import GapDiagram from '../components/charts/GapDiagram.astro';
import DecompositionFigure from '../components/charts/DecompositionFigure.astro';

const cfs = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'on-CPU vs schedstat')!;
---

<Base
  title="About — PACE"
  description="One maintainer, one open repository, and a free instance of the analyzer running on a free host. What that means for you, including what it does not."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">About</div>
      <h1>One maintainer, one repository, and a service that costs nothing to use</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        PACE is built by <strong>{site.author}</strong>. It is not a company, there is no commercial
        licence to buy, and there is nothing on this site that is gated behind one. The library, the
        analyzer and the service are all here, and the fastest way to get an answer about any of them
        is to email me directly.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        The reason it is built this way is simple: a measurement is only worth as much as somebody
        else's ability to check it. A library you cannot read the source of, and a number you cannot
        re-run, is a claim. Everything about how this project is published follows from trying not to
        make claims.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href={`mailto:${site.contact}`}>
          <i class="ph ph-envelope-simple"></i>
          {site.contact}
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={site.repo}>
          <i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i>
          The repository
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">What the project is for</div>
      <h2>A wrong optimisation is expensive; knowing which one not to make is cheap</h2>
      <p>
        The whole reason this exists is the figure below. A thread that lost a large part of its wall
        time to the scheduler is not a code problem, and every hour spent optimising the code inside
        it is an hour spent on nothing. That mistake is invisible to a CPU-time counter, which is why
        it gets made.
      </p>
      <GapDiagram />
      <DecompositionFigure />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">How it is published</div>
      <h2>Source-available, free to use, and verifiable</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i> Everything is in the open</h3>
          <p>
            The library, the analyzer, the verification harness, the raw measurement artifacts, the
            review documents that corrected the positioning, and the scripts that fail the build when
            a number on this site drifts from a measurement.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one thing worth reading before anything else is
            {' '}<a href={`${site.repo}/blob/main/docs/ENG-REVIEW.md`}>the engineering review</a>: it
            records the two defects the verification harness found in the product itself, which is a
            more useful document than a changelog.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-scales"></i> The licences, without euphemism</h3>
          <p>
            The analyzer is <strong>Apache-2.0</strong> — OSI open source, no conditions beyond
            attribution, and it is deliberately the free half: it is how you check the method before
            you trust anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            The library is <strong>BUSL-1.1</strong>, which is source-available rather than open
            source: reading it, modifying it and developing against it are free, production use of a
            shipped product needs terms, and it converts to Apache-2.0 on 2030-11-01. Full text
            on <a href={href('license/')}>the licence page</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The hosted service</div>
      <h2>Why there is one, and exactly what it is</h2>
      <p>
        The analyzer is a Rust binary. To see what PACE says about a trace you would otherwise need a
        toolchain, a clone and a build, which is a lot of commitment for one question. So the same
        binary runs behind an HTTP endpoint that anybody can call without an account.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-cloud"></i> One instance, on a free host</h3>
          <p>
            A single free instance with a tenth of a core and half a gigabyte of memory. It sleeps
            when idle and takes about a minute to wake, and an uptime monitor pings it every five
            minutes so that it usually does not.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-database-slash"></i> Nothing is stored</h3>
          <p>
            The trace is read into memory for one request and then dropped. There is no database and
            no persistent disk on the host, so there is nowhere for it to be kept even by accident.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-code"></i> It is the same code</h3>
          <p>
            The container definition and the host's own service manifest are in the repository. You
            can deploy the identical thing yourself, and that is the intended path if you use this
            regularly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">What the free instance means in practice</div>
        <p>
          A large trace will time out here and would not on your machine, and one request is served at
          a time. Neither is a property of the measurement. If you hit either, run
          {' '}<span class="mono">pace analyze</span> locally — it is one cargo build, and it is the
          tool the service is calling.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Maintenance, honestly</div>
      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <div class="card-row">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-user"></i> Not a team</h3>
          <p>
            One person. Issues get answered, but not on a service-level agreement, and there is no
            support contract to buy. If the repository looks abandoned, it is probably a week of
            something else and an email will resolve it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-buildings"></i> Not a business</h3>
          <p>
            There is no pricing page, no sales motion and no paid tier. This is a measurement
            published with the code that produces it, and the hosting is free because the free tier
            of a host is enough for it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-flask"></i> A measurement with an author</h3>
          <p>
            Every figure was produced on one machine by one person. The artifacts, the harness and the
            environment are all published so you can decide how much weight that deserves — and so
            that re-running it on your hardware is a command rather than a request.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The tightest published agreement, for reference: on-CPU time against the kernel's own
        accounting at {fine(cfs.median)} median, on {environment.cpu_model} running
        {' '}<span class="mono">{environment.uname}</span>, whose source is
        {' '}<a href={`${site.repo}/blob/main/pace-sdk/results/accuracy.json`}>the committed
        artifact</a> and not this paragraph.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
